INT. BOOKBINDERY - EDWARDIAN LONDON - MORNING

A narrow workshop, three floors above a cobbled lane. Presses of black iron and polished oak. Racks of calf and morocco leather in every shade of brown. Gold leaf trembles on its tissue by the window. Sunlight falls on piles of unbound folios, tied with string.

MR. EDWIN ASHCROFT, 60s, the master binder, stands at the bench in a leather apron, his spectacles pushed up on his forehead. Grey whiskers, ink-stained thumbs. He cradles a half-bound volume with reverent hands.

MISS ADELAIDE FOX, 20s, his new apprentice, in a plain grey dress with the sleeves rolled back, stands rigid at the opposite end. Her hair is pinned beneath a cap. She has been up since four.

ASHCROFT
Miss Fox.

ADELAIDE
Yes, Mr. Ashcroft.

ASHCROFT
You have been with me three weeks.

ADELAIDE
Yes, sir.

ASHCROFT
In three weeks you have sewn twelve signatures, trimmed four boards, and burned the edge of a very costly sheepskin.

ADELAIDE
I am sorry about the sheepskin.

ASHCROFT
You should be.

He lays the volume on the bench. Opens it. Turns three pages. Stops.

ASHCROFT (CONT'D)
This is yours.

ADELAIDE
My stitching?

ASHCROFT
Your stitching.

She steps forward. Her breath quickens. He taps the spine.

ASHCROFT (CONT'D)
Uneven.

ADELAIDE
I know, sir.

ASHCROFT
Here. And here.

ADELAIDE
I counted forty. I only had thirty-seven.

ASHCROFT
You counted forty because you hurried.

ADELAIDE
Yes, sir.

ASHCROFT
Why?

ADELAIDE
Because I was afraid you'd send me away.

He considers her. Takes off his spectacles. Folds them. Slips them into his apron pocket.

ASHCROFT
Miss Fox, this volume is a Bible. It was commissioned by the Bishop of Winchester. It shall sit on an altar for two hundred years.

ADELAIDE
Then I must unpick it.

ASHCROFT
You must unpick it.

ADELAIDE
Now?

ASHCROFT
Now.

She takes a needle. A pair of fine shears. She bends over the volume. Her hands shake once and then do not.

ASHCROFT (CONT'D)
Slower.

ADELAIDE
Yes, sir.

ASHCROFT
Do not hurry what will outlive you.

She snips a thread. Another. The room is silent save for the tick of a pocket-watch on the shelf.

ASHCROFT (CONT'D)
When you finish, you will sew it again.

ADELAIDE
Properly.

ASHCROFT
Properly. And if it is not properly done you will unpick it again.

ADELAIDE
How many times?

ASHCROFT
As many as it takes.

Adelaide looks up. For the first time, she sees something in his face she had not expected: a small, grim, unmistakable kindness.

ADELAIDE
Thank you, sir.

ASHCROFT
Do not thank me. Thread your needle.
